=== Q001
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Opening inventory is $4,000, purchases are $30,000 and closing inventory is $6,000. What is cost of sales ($)?
answer: 28000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost of sales = opening inventory + purchases - closing inventory
step: 4,000 + 30,000 = 34,000.
step: 34,000 - 6,000 = $28,000.
check: 4000+30000-6000
explain: Closing inventory is carried forward as an asset.
mistake: Adding closing inventory.
tip: Plus opening, minus closing.
src: Study text ch5 - cost of sales (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Under IAS 2, at what amount is inventory measured?
+ The lower of cost and net realisable value || Prudent measurement.
- The higher of cost and NRV || Would overstate assets.
- Selling price || Includes unearned profit.
- Replacement cost always || Not the IAS 2 rule.
explain: The comparison is made item by item.
mistake: Using selling price.
tip: Lower of cost and NRV.
src: Study text ch5 - IAS 2 (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which cost formula is NOT permitted by IAS 2?
+ LIFO (last in, first out) || Prohibited.
- FIFO || Permitted.
- Periodic weighted average || Permitted.
- Continuous weighted average || Permitted.
explain: LIFO can understate inventory in rising prices.
mistake: Thinking LIFO is allowed.
tip: No LIFO.
src: Study text ch5 - cost formulas (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which concept explains why unsold inventory is carried forward rather than expensed?
+ Matching (accruals) - costs are matched with the revenue they help earn || Expense when sold.
- Going concern only || Not the main concept.
- Business entity || Not relevant.
- Materiality || Not relevant.
explain: Cost of sales includes only goods actually sold.
mistake: Expensing all purchases.
tip: Match cost to sale.
src: Study text ch5 - matching (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What is the year-end entry to record closing inventory?
+ Debit inventory; credit cost of sales || Asset recognised, cost of sales reduced.
- Debit cost of sales; credit inventory || That transfers opening inventory.
- Debit purchases; credit inventory || Not correct.
- Debit inventory; credit sales || Not revenue.
explain: Opening inventory is transferred: Dr cost of sales, Cr inventory.
mistake: Reversing the entry.
tip: Closing inventory = asset debit.
src: Study text ch5 - inventory entries (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How is net realisable value (NRV) calculated?
+ Estimated selling price less costs to complete and costs to sell || Net amount realisable.
- Cost plus a mark-up || Not NRV.
- Replacement cost less depreciation || Not NRV.
- Selling price plus selling costs || Wrong sign.
explain: If NRV falls below cost, inventory is written down.
mistake: Adding selling costs.
tip: Price less costs to finish and sell.
src: Study text ch5 - NRV (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What does FIFO assume?
+ The oldest items are sold first || Closing inventory is the most recent purchases.
- The newest items are sold first || LIFO.
- All items cost the same average price || AVCO.
- Items are sold at random || Not a method.
explain: In rising prices FIFO gives the highest closing inventory.
mistake: Confusing with LIFO.
tip: First in, first out.
src: Study text ch5 - FIFO (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: An item cost $40. It can be sold for $50 after completion costs of $8 and selling costs of $5. At what value ($) should it be included in inventory?
answer: 37
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Value = lower of cost and (selling price - completion - selling costs)
step: NRV = 50 - 8 - 5 = 37.
step: Lower of cost 40 and NRV 37 = $37.
check: 50-8-5
explain: A write-down of $3 is charged to profit.
mistake: Ignoring completion costs.
tip: Compare cost with NRV.
src: Study text ch5 - NRV (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which cost should be INCLUDED in the cost of inventory under IAS 2?
+ Import duties on raw materials || Part of purchase cost.
- Selling costs || Excluded.
- General administrative overheads || Excluded.
- Abnormal wastage || Excluded.
explain: Cost includes bringing inventory to its present location and condition.
mistake: Including selling costs.
tip: Getting it here and ready.
src: Study text ch5 - cost (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Sales are $45,000 and cost of sales is $28,000. What is gross profit ($)?
answer: 17000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Gross profit = sales - cost of sales
step: Sales = 45,000; cost of sales = 28,000.
step: Gross profit = $17,000.
check: 45000-28000
explain: Closing inventory affects gross profit through cost of sales.
mistake: Subtracting purchases.
tip: Sales less cost of sales.
src: Study text ch5 - gross profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Item A: cost $2,000, NRV $2,600. Item B: cost $3,500, NRV $2,900. Item C: cost $1,800, NRV $2,100.
q: At what total value ($) should inventory be stated?
answer: 6700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Inventory = Σ lower of cost and NRV, item by item
step: A 2,000; B 2,900; C 1,800.
step: Total = 2,000 + 2,900 + 1,800 = $6,700.
check: 2000+2900+1800
explain: Comparing totals (cost 7,300 vs NRV 7,600) would wrongly give 7,300.
mistake: Comparing in total.
tip: Item by item.
src: Study text ch5 - item by item (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Purchase price of goods $20,000; import duty $1,500; carriage inwards $800; trade discount received $1,000; storage at the warehouse after arrival $400 (not necessary for production).
q: What is the cost ($) of the inventory under IAS 2?
answer: 21300
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost = purchase price + duties + transport - trade discounts
step: 20,000 + 1,500 + 800 = 22,300.
step: Less trade discount 1,000 = $21,300 (storage excluded).
check: 20000+1500+800-1000
explain: Storage after arrival is not part of cost unless needed in production.
mistake: Including storage.
tip: Bring it here; don't store it.
src: Study text ch5 - cost (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q013
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Opening inventory 100 units at $10. Purchases: 200 units at $12 in January; 150 units at $13 in March. 220 units were sold in February. Closing inventory 230 units.
q: What is the closing inventory value using FIFO?
+ $2,910 || 150 x 13 + 80 x 12.
- $2,734 || Periodic AVCO.
- $2,857 || Continuous AVCO.
- $2,440 || FIFO cost of sales.
formula: FIFO closing = most recent purchases
step: Latest 150 units at 13 = 1,950.
step: Next 80 units from January at 12 = 960; total = $2,910.
check: 150*13+80*12
explain: FIFO leaves the newest costs in inventory.
mistake: Valuing at opening prices.
tip: Closing = latest layers.
src: Study text ch5 - FIFO (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q014
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Opening inventory 100 units at $10. Purchases: 200 units at $12; 150 units at $13. Closing inventory 230 units.
q: Using the PERIODIC weighted average method, what is closing inventory ($)? Round to the nearest dollar.
answer: 2734
tol: 0.5
unit: $
formula: Average cost = (opening + purchases) / total units; closing = units x average
step: Total cost = 1,000 + 2,400 + 1,950 = 5,350; units = 450; average = $11.889.
step: Closing = 230 x 11.889 = $2,734.
check: 230*5350/450
explain: One average is used for the whole period.
mistake: Averaging the three prices without weighting.
tip: Weight by units.
src: Study text ch5 - periodic AVCO (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q015
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO costs should be EXCLUDED from the cost of inventory?
+ Selling costs || Excluded.
+ Abnormal amounts of wasted materials || Excluded.
- Direct labour in production || Conversion cost.
- Production overheads based on normal capacity || Conversion cost.
explain: Administrative overheads and unnecessary storage are also excluded.
mistake: Including selling costs.
tip: Production yes; selling no.
src: Study text ch5 - exclusions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Match each cost formula with its description.
pair: FIFO => Oldest items are assumed sold first
pair: Periodic weighted average => One average cost calculated for the whole period
pair: Continuous weighted average => A new average calculated after each purchase
pair: LIFO => Not permitted under IAS 2
explain: Different methods give different profits.
mistake: Mixing the average methods.
tip: Period vs continuous.
src: Study text ch5 - cost formulas (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Inventory cost $15,000 but its NRV is now $11,500. What write-down ($) is charged to profit or loss?
answer: 3500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Write-down = cost - NRV
step: 15,000 - 11,500.
step: = $3,500.
check: 15000-11500
explain: The write-down increases cost of sales.
mistake: Writing up to NRV when NRV exceeds cost.
tip: Only write down.
src: Study text ch5 - write-down (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: In a period of RISING prices, which method gives the HIGHEST closing inventory value?
+ FIFO || Closing inventory at latest, higher prices.
- Periodic weighted average || Between.
- Continuous weighted average || Between.
- All give the same value || They differ.
explain: Higher closing inventory gives higher profit.
mistake: Thinking methods agree.
tip: FIFO = recent prices.
src: Study text ch5 - comparison (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Per unit: direct materials $6; direct labour $4; production overheads (at normal capacity) $2; administration overhead $1; selling costs $1.50.
q: What is the cost per unit ($) for inventory valuation?
answer: 12
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost = materials + labour + production overheads
step: 6 + 4 + 2 = 12.
step: Administration and selling costs are excluded.
check: 6+4+2
explain: Conversion costs include production overheads only.
mistake: Including admin or selling costs.
tip: Production costs only.
src: Study text ch5 - conversion cost (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Where does closing inventory appear in the financial statements?
+ As a current asset in the SOFP, and deducted in cost of sales || Both statements.
- As a non-current asset || Current.
- As an expense only || It reduces cost of sales.
- As a liability || An asset.
explain: Opening inventory is added in cost of sales.
mistake: Showing it only in one statement.
tip: SOFP asset + COS deduction.
src: Study text ch5 - presentation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Closing inventory is overstated by $2,000. What is the effect on profit for the year?
+ Profit is overstated by $2,000 || Cost of sales is understated.
- Profit is understated by $2,000 || Overstated.
- No effect || Profit is affected.
- Profit is overstated by $4,000 || Only once.
formula: Cost of sales = opening + purchases - closing
step: Higher closing inventory lowers cost of sales by 2,000.
step: Profit rises by 2,000.
check: 2000
explain: Next year's opening inventory will also be overstated, reducing next year's profit.
mistake: Reversing the effect.
tip: Closing up = profit up.
src: Study text ch5 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Why is the cost and NRV comparison made item by item?
+ Losses on some items must not be hidden by gains on others || Prudence.
- To save time || It takes longer.
- Because IAS 2 bans totals for convenience || The reason is prudence.
- To increase inventory value || It reduces it.
explain: Groups of similar items may be compared together.
mistake: Comparing totals.
tip: Don't offset.
src: Study text ch5 - item by item (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Opening inventory 100 units at $10. January: purchased 200 units at $12. February: sold 220 units at $20 each.
q: Using FIFO, what is the gross profit ($) on the February sales?
answer: 1960
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Gross profit = sales - FIFO cost of sales
step: FIFO cost = 100 x 10 + 120 x 12 = 1,000 + 1,440 = 2,440.
step: Sales = 220 x 20 = 4,400; GP = 4,400 - 2,440 = $1,960.
check: 220*20-(100*10+120*12)
explain: FIFO uses the oldest costs first.
mistake: Using the latest price for all units.
tip: Old layers first.
src: Study text ch5 - FIFO profit (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which of these is a reason NRV might fall BELOW cost?
+ The goods have become damaged or obsolete || Selling price falls.
- Purchase prices have risen || Raises replacement cost, not NRV.
- Demand has increased || Usually raises price.
- Selling costs have fallen || Increases NRV.
explain: Errors in production or marketing decisions also reduce NRV.
mistake: Linking NRV to purchase price.
tip: Damaged, obsolete, falling price.
src: Study text ch5 - NRV (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What is the difference between periodic and continuous inventory records?
+ Periodic records count inventory at the period end; continuous records update after each movement || Timing of records.
- Periodic records are not allowed || Allowed.
- Continuous records never need counting || Counts still verify.
- There is no difference || There is.
explain: Continuous AVCO recalculates after each purchase.
mistake: Thinking counts are unnecessary.
tip: End vs every movement.
src: Study text ch5 - records (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee values inventory at selling price "because that is what it is worth". What is wrong?
+ Inventory is valued at the lower of cost and NRV; selling price would recognise profit before sale || Prudence.
- Nothing is wrong || Overstates.
- Inventory should be at zero || Not correct.
- Inventory should be at higher of cost and NRV || Not correct.
explain: Profit is recognised only when goods are sold.
mistake: Using selling price.
tip: Never above cost.
src: Study text ch5 - valuation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Put the steps of calculating gross profit in order.
item: Start with opening inventory
item: Add purchases for the period
item: Deduct closing inventory to find cost of sales
item: Deduct cost of sales from revenue
explain: This is the cost of sales working.
mistake: Adding closing inventory.
tip: Opening, purchases, closing, revenue.
src: Study text ch5 - gross profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What is the year-end entry that transfers OPENING inventory?
+ Debit cost of sales; credit inventory || Opening inventory becomes an expense.
- Debit inventory; credit cost of sales || That records closing inventory.
- Debit purchases; credit inventory || Not standard.
- No entry || An entry is needed.
explain: Then closing inventory is recognised.
mistake: Reversing the entries.
tip: Opening out, closing in.
src: Study text ch5 - inventory entries (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How are production overheads allocated to inventory under IAS 2?
+ Based on the normal capacity of production facilities || Avoids inflating cost in low-output periods.
- Based on actual output, even if very low || Could overstate cost.
- Not at all || They are included.
- Based on sales volume || Not correct.
explain: Unallocated overheads in low-output periods are expensed.
mistake: Allocating all overheads over low output.
tip: Normal capacity.
src: Study text ch5 - overheads (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Cost of sales is $52,000, opening inventory $7,500 and closing inventory $9,200. What were purchases ($)?
answer: 53700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Purchases = cost of sales - opening + closing
step: 52,000 - 7,500 = 44,500.
step: 44,500 + 9,200 = $53,700.
check: 52000-7500+9200
explain: Rearrange the cost of sales formula.
mistake: Reversing opening and closing.
tip: Work back carefully.
src: Study text ch5 - cost of sales (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Opening inventory 100 units at $10. January: purchased 200 units at $12. February: sold 220 units. March: purchased 150 units at $13.
q: Using the CONTINUOUS weighted average method, what is closing inventory ($)? Round to the nearest dollar.
answer: 2857
tol: 0.5
unit: $
formula: New average after each purchase
step: After January: 300 units costing 3,400; average 11.333. Sale of 220 leaves 80 units = 906.67.
step: After March: 906.67 + 1,950 = 2,856.67 for 230 units = $2,857.
check: 3400/300*80+150*13
explain: The average is recalculated after each purchase.
mistake: Using one average for the period.
tip: Recalculate after every purchase.
src: Study text ch5 - continuous AVCO (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Inventory includes 500 units costing $30 each. They are damaged; after repairs costing $4 per unit they can be sold for $35 each, with selling commission of 10% of the selling price.
q: What value should be included for these units?
+ $13,750 || NRV 35 - 4 - 3.50 = 27.50 x 500.
- $15,000 || At cost.
- $15,500 || Ignores commission.
- $17,500 || At selling price.
formula: Value = units x lower of cost and NRV
step: NRV per unit = 35 - 4 - 3.50 = 27.50.
step: Lower than cost $30; value = 500 x 27.50 = $13,750.
check: 500*(35-4-35*0.1)
explain: The write-down is 500 x 2.50 = $1,250.
mistake: Forgetting commission.
tip: All costs to sell.
src: Study text ch5 - NRV (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q033
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Sales $120,000; opening inventory $14,000; purchases $78,000; carriage inwards $2,000; returns outwards $3,000; closing inventory $16,500.
q: What is gross profit ($)?
answer: 45500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: GP = sales - (opening + purchases + carriage in - returns out - closing)
step: Cost of sales = 14,000 + 78,000 + 2,000 - 3,000 - 16,500 = 74,500.
step: GP = 120,000 - 74,500 = $45,500.
check: 120000-(14000+78000+2000-3000-16500)
explain: Carriage inwards is part of cost of purchases.
mistake: Treating carriage inwards as a distribution expense.
tip: Carriage IN = cost of sales.
src: Study text ch5 - cost of sales (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q034
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Item X: cost $5,000, NRV $4,200. Item Y: cost $3,000, NRV $4,500.
q: A trainee compares totals (cost $8,000, NRV $8,700) and values inventory at $8,000. What is the correct value?
+ $7,200 || 4,200 + 3,000 item by item.
- $8,000 || Total comparison.
- $8,700 || Total NRV.
- $7,500 || Not supported.
formula: Σ lower of cost and NRV per item
step: X: lower of 5,000 and 4,200 = 4,200.
step: Y: lower of 3,000 and 4,500 = 3,000; total $7,200.
check: 4200+3000
explain: The gain on Y must not offset the loss on X.
mistake: Total comparison.
tip: Item by item.
src: Study text ch5 - item by item (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: Prices have risen steadily all year. A company switches from AVCO to FIFO. What is the likely effect on reported profit this year?
+ Profit increases, because closing inventory is valued at higher recent prices || Lower cost of sales.
- Profit decreases || Opposite.
- No effect || Methods differ.
- Revenue increases || Revenue unaffected.
explain: Consistent use of methods supports comparability.
mistake: Thinking methods only affect inventory.
tip: Higher closing = higher profit.
src: Study text ch5 - method effects (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A business has a gross profit margin of 25% on sales of $80,000. Opening inventory is $9,000 and purchases are $63,000. What is closing inventory ($)?
answer: 12000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Closing = opening + purchases - cost of sales
step: Cost of sales = 80,000 x 75% = 60,000.
step: Closing = 9,000 + 63,000 - 60,000 = $12,000.
check: 9000+63000-80000*0.75
explain: Useful for estimating inventory lost in a fire or theft.
mistake: Using a 25% mark-up instead of margin.
tip: Margin on sales.
src: Study text ch5 - incomplete records link (concept basis; original question)

=== Q037
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO must be disclosed about inventories under IAS 2?
+ The accounting policies, including the cost formula used || Required.
+ The amount of any write-down recognised as an expense || Required.
- The names of all suppliers || Not required.
- The selling price of every item || Not required.
explain: Carrying amounts by classification are also disclosed.
mistake: Over-disclosing commercial detail.
tip: Policy, amounts, write-downs.
src: Study text ch5 - disclosure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Opening inventory 50 units at $8. Purchases: 100 units at $9; 150 units at $10. Sales: 220 units at $15.
q: Using FIFO, what is gross profit ($)?
answer: 1300
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: GP = sales - FIFO cost of sales
step: Cost of 220 units = 50 x 8 + 100 x 9 + 70 x 10 = 400 + 900 + 700 = 2,000.
step: Sales = 220 x 15 = 3,300; GP = 3,300 - 2,000 = $1,300.
check: 220*15-(50*8+100*9+70*10)
explain: Closing inventory is 80 units at $10 = $800.
mistake: Using the average price for all units.
tip: Use up the oldest layers first.
src: Study text ch5 - FIFO (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Inventory was written down to NRV last year. This year the selling price has recovered. What does IAS 2 allow?
+ The write-down is reversed, but the new carrying amount cannot exceed original cost || Limited reversal.
- Inventory is written up to the new selling price || Above cost is not allowed.
- No reversal is ever permitted || Reversal is permitted.
- The reversal goes to share capital || Goes to profit or loss.
explain: The reversal reduces the inventory expense of the period.
mistake: Writing up above cost.
tip: Back up to cost, no further.
src: Study text ch5 - reversals (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: The year end is 31 December. Inventory was counted on 3 January at cost of $21,000. Between 1 and 3 January, goods costing $1,500 were sold and goods costing $800 were received from suppliers.
q: What is the inventory value ($) at 31 December?
answer: 21700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Year-end inventory = count + goods sold since - goods received since
step: Add back goods sold after the year end: 21,000 + 1,500 = 22,500.
step: Remove goods received after the year end: 22,500 - 800 = $21,700.
check: 21000+1500-800
explain: Work back from the count date to the reporting date.
mistake: Reversing the adjustments.
tip: Undo post year-end movements.
src: Study text ch5 - inventory counts (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q041
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee adds showroom display and advertising costs to the cost of finished goods inventory. What is wrong?
+ Selling costs are excluded from inventory cost under IAS 2 || Expensed as incurred.
- Nothing is wrong || They are selling costs.
- They should be added to NRV || NRV deducts selling costs.
- They should be capitalised as non-current assets || Not assets.
explain: Including them overstates inventory and profit.
mistake: Capitalising selling costs.
tip: Selling costs = expense.
src: Study text ch5 - exclusions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Opening inventory 100 units at $10. Purchases: 200 units at $12; 150 units at $13. 220 units were sold in the period.
q: Using the PERIODIC weighted average method, what is cost of sales (to the nearest dollar)?
+ $2,616 || 220 x 5,350 / 450.
- $2,440 || FIFO.
- $2,734 || Closing inventory.
- $2,640 || Uses $12 for all units.
formula: Cost of sales = units sold x period average cost
step: Average = 5,350 / 450 = 11.889.
step: Cost of sales = 220 x 11.889 = $2,616.
check: 220*5350/450
explain: Cost of sales + closing inventory = 2,616 + 2,734 = 5,350.
mistake: Using one purchase price.
tip: Units sold x average.
src: Study text ch5 - periodic AVCO (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Does the choice between FIFO and AVCO affect the company's cash flows?
+ No - it changes reported cost of sales and inventory, not cash paid to suppliers || Accounting allocation only.
- Yes - FIFO always increases cash || Cash is unaffected (ignoring tax).
- Yes - AVCO always reduces cash || Cash is unaffected.
- Only for cash sales || No.
explain: The method allocates the same total cost between periods.
mistake: Confusing profit and cash.
tip: Method = allocation, not cash.
src: Study text ch5 - methods (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Last year's closing inventory was overstated by $1,500 and the error was not corrected. What is the effect on THIS year's profit?
+ Understated by $1,500 || Opening inventory too high increases this year's cost of sales.
- Overstated by $1,500 || That was last year.
- No effect || Opening inventory is affected.
- Understated by $3,000 || Only once.
formula: Cost of sales = opening + purchases - closing
step: Opening inventory is overstated by 1,500.
step: Cost of sales is overstated, so profit is understated by 1,500.
check: 1500
explain: Inventory errors reverse in the next period.
mistake: Thinking the error repeats in the same direction.
tip: Last year up, this year down.
src: Study text ch5 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: For a manufacturer, which items are included in inventory?
+ Raw materials, work in progress and finished goods || All three.
- Finished goods only || Not complete.
- Machinery used in production || Non-current asset.
- Office stationery for admin use || Usually expensed.
explain: Each is measured at the lower of cost and NRV.
mistake: Leaving out WIP.
tip: Raw, WIP, finished.
src: Study text ch5 - inventory types (concept basis; original question)

=== Q046
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Fixed production overheads are $60,000 and normal capacity is 20,000 units, but only 15,000 units were produced. Direct materials are $5 and direct labour $4 per unit.
q: What is the cost per unit ($) for inventory valuation?
answer: 12
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost = materials + labour + fixed overhead / normal capacity
step: Overhead per unit = 60,000 / 20,000 = 3.
step: Cost = 5 + 4 + 3 = $12.
check: 5+4+60000/20000
explain: Unabsorbed overhead (5,000 x 3 = 15,000) is expensed.
mistake: Dividing by actual output (giving $4).
tip: Normal capacity, not actual.
src: Study text ch5 - overheads (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q047
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: When prices are FALLING, which method gives the LOWEST closing inventory value?
+ FIFO || Closing inventory is at the latest, lower prices.
- Periodic weighted average || Between.
- Continuous weighted average || Between.
- All give the same value || They differ.
explain: The effects reverse when the price trend reverses.
mistake: Assuming FIFO always gives the highest value.
tip: FIFO follows recent prices.
src: Study text ch5 - falling prices (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A retailer holds 1,000 units of an outdated phone model costing $80 each. They can now only be sold to a recycler for $5 each, with collection costs of $1 per unit. At what amount should they be included in inventory?
+ $4,000 || NRV 5 - 1 = 4 per unit.
- $80,000 || At cost.
- $5,000 || Ignores collection costs.
- $0 || They still have some value.
formula: Value = units x lower of cost and NRV
step: NRV = 5 - 1 = $4 per unit.
step: Value = 1,000 x 4 = $4,000.
check: 1000*(5-1)
explain: The write-down is $76,000.
mistake: Leaving obsolete stock at cost.
tip: Obsolete = write down to NRV.
src: Study text ch5 - obsolescence (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which statement about IAS 2 is correct?
+ Inventory is measured at the lower of cost and NRV, item by item || Core rule.
- Inventory is measured at selling price || Not allowed.
- LIFO is the preferred method || Not permitted.
- Selling costs are included in cost || Excluded.
explain: FIFO and weighted average are permitted.
mistake: Comparing in total.
tip: Lower of cost and NRV, item by item.
src: Study text ch5 - IAS 2 (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t1
status: review
important: 1
q: Which formula gives cost of sales?
+ Opening inventory + purchases - closing inventory || Core formula.
- Opening inventory - purchases + closing inventory || Signs wrong.
- Purchases + closing inventory || Wrong.
- Sales - purchases || That is not cost of sales.
explain: Carriage inwards and returns outwards adjust purchases.
mistake: Adding closing inventory.
tip: Plus opening, minus closing.
src: Study text ch5 - cost of sales (concept basis; original question)
